Golden Signals Operational Dashboard Layout Design Report
1. Golden Signals Architectural Mapping
1. Latency
Metric: End-to-end HTTP request duration using P50, P90, and P99 percentiles.
Measurement: P99 latency is calculated from http_request_duration_seconds_bucket.
Success versus error: Latency should be separated by service and, where available, HTTP status so successful and failed requests can be compared.
Thresholds: Below 400 ms is nominal, 400–800 ms is warning, and 800 ms or more is critical.
2. Traffic
Metric: HTTP requests per second grouped by service.
Measurement: Traffic is calculated using the one-minute rate of http_requests_total.
Unit of demand: Requests per second.
Thresholds: A 30% deviation from baseline is a warning, while a 60% deviation is critical.
3. Errors
Metric: Percentage of HTTP requests returning 5xx status codes.
Measurement: The 5xx request rate is divided by the total request rate and multiplied by 100.
Classification: Errors are explicitly identified using HTTP statuses matching 5...
Thresholds: Below 0.5% is nominal, 0.5–2% is warning, and 2% or more is critical.
4. Saturation
Metric: Cluster compute and memory utilization.
Measurement: Average resource utilization percentage grouped by resource.
Leading indicators: High CPU or memory utilization can indicate capacity exhaustion before service failures occur.
Thresholds: Below 75% is nominal, 75–85% is warning, and 85% or more is critical.
2. Visual Layout Hierarchy and Information Flow

The dashboard uses four tiers to support fast NOC triage. Operators begin with global service health and move downward into increasingly detailed diagnostics.

Tier 1: Executive Overview and Global Service Health

Panels:

Overall SLO Compliance
Active P1/P2 Incidents
Global Request Traffic

Panel types: Stat panels.

Operational purpose: Provides an immediate overview of business service health. Leadership and NOC operators can quickly determine whether SLOs are being met, whether major incidents are active, and whether global traffic is normal.

Tier 2: The Four Golden Signals

Panels:

End-to-End Latency Percentiles
HTTP Throughput by Service
HTTP 5xx Error Rate
Cluster Compute and Memory Saturation

Panel types: Time-series panels and gauge.

Operational purpose: Answers the four initial incident questions:

Is the platform slow?
Is traffic increasing or decreasing?
Is the platform failing?
Are resources becoming full?
Tier 3: Component and Dependency Breakdown

Panels:

Database Connection Pool Utilization
External Third-Party API Response Times

Panel types: Bar gauge and time series.

Operational purpose: Identifies whether degradation originates from database connection exhaustion or a slow external provider.

Tier 4: Deep-Dive Infrastructure Diagnostics

Panels:

JVM Garbage Collection Pause Time
Host Disk I/O Queue Depth

Panel type: Time series.

Operational purpose: Supports infrastructure engineers during detailed investigation of runtime and host-level performance issues.

