Additional thresholds include:

Active incidents: zero is green, one is warning, and two or more are critical.
External API latency: below 300 ms is green, 300–1000 ms is warning, and 1000 ms or more is critical.
JVM GC pause: below 0.1 seconds is green, 0.1–0.5 seconds is warning, and 0.5 seconds or more is critical.
Disk I/O queue depth: below 2 is green, 2–5 is warning, and 5 or more is critical.

The dashboard should use green for normal operation, yellow or orange for warning, and red for critical conditions.

4. Cognitive Ergonomics and Anti-Patterns Avoided
1. Metric Clutter Avoidance

Principle: Only high-priority metrics are displayed in the main dashboard.

Executive indicators are placed at the top, while component and infrastructure metrics appear in lower tiers. This prevents operators from being overwhelmed by low-level data during initial triage.

2. Consistent Color Coding

Principle: The same health colors are used across all panels.

Green indicates normal operation.
Yellow or orange indicates warning.
Red indicates critical degradation.

Consistent colors help operators recognize severity without reading every value.

3. Standardized Time Windows

Principle: Related metrics use consistent rate windows and the dashboard refreshes every 15 seconds.

Short windows such as one minute support rapid traffic monitoring, while five-minute windows reduce noise for latency and error-rate calculations. Consistent time ranges prevent misleading comparisons.

Anti-Patterns Avoided
Too many unrelated panels in one row
Using different colors for the same severity
Mixing executive and infrastructure metrics
Displaying raw counters instead of rates
Using gauges for historical trends
Creating alerts without clear thresholds
Placing detailed metrics above global service health
Conclusion

The dashboard organizes operational information into four levels: global health, Golden Signals, component dependencies, and infrastructure diagnostics. This structure allows NOC operators and SRE incident commanders to move from detection to root-cause analysis quickly.

The dashboard measures latency, traffic, errors, and saturation using suitable visualizations and clearly defined green, warning, and critical thresholds. The 15-second refresh cadence supports active monitoring, while the hierarchical layout reduces cognitive load and alert fatigue.

